.TH PRELUDE 8 2026-10-19 "Peios prelude 0.0.7"
.SH NAME
prelude \- prepare the initramfs and hand off to the real-root init
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /init
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B prelude
runs as process 1 in the initramfs. It mounts the kernel virtual filesystems,
seeds device-node security descriptors, runs boot hooks, and switches to the
root filesystem mounted by those hooks at
.IR /mnt/rootfs .
It then replaces itself with the real-root init.
.PP
Prelude is a boot entry point, not a command to start on a running system.
Its mount changes and old-root cleanup affect the boot environment.
.SH OPTIONS
Prelude reads these parameters from
.IR /proc/cmdline ,
not from its process arguments.
.TP
.BI init= path
Try this executable on the mounted root before the fallback chain.
The last nonempty value wins. An unsuccessful exec proceeds to the next
candidate. Fallbacks are /bin/peinit2, /sbin/init, /bin/init, and /bin/sh,
in that order.
.TP
.B peios.quiet=2
Suppress Prelude progress, success messages, and its banner after the command
line has been read. Warnings and failures remain visible. The last value
wins; other values do not suppress Prelude output. Hook output is independent.
.TP
.B rd.shell
Open a recovery shell after a boot failure. Exiting that shell proceeds
to the halt path.
.TP
.BR rd.break [ =\fIhook\fR [ ,\fIhook\fR ...]]
Pause before reading the hook sequence, or before the named hooks.
Names match a complete hook path or its final filename. Repeated parameters
accumulate. Exiting the shell resumes boot; a deferred hook can reach the
same named breakpoint again. Any breakpoint also enables the failure shell.
.TP
.B TERM=dumb
Disable Prelude console color. The last TERM value wins; other values enable
color. Executed hooks and init candidates receive TERM=linux independently.
.SH SECURITY
Prelude runs with the boot process's authority. Its hooks execute with that
authority and can mount filesystems and change the environment before handoff.
Control of hook files or recovery-shell access therefore grants control of
boot. This component does not verify an image's signature.
.PP
Prelude invokes seed-sd recursively on /dev. Failure produces a warning and
boot continues. See
.BR seed-sd (8)
for the descriptor and required access.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Successful handoff replaces the process and does not return. On failure,
Prelude reports the error, optionally runs the recovery shell, synchronizes
filesystems, and requests a system halt. If halt returns, process 1 parks
instead of exiting.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Hooks and the recovery shell receive PATH=/usr/bin and TERM=linux.
Real-root init candidates receive TERM=linux, with no PATH supplied.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /system/prelude/hooks.seq.1
Version 1 hook sequence.
.TP
.I /system/prelude/hooks.seq.2
Version 2 hook sequence with runtime capability declarations.
.TP
.I /hooks.seq
Legacy version 1 sequence, selected only when no supported numbered sequence
is available.
.TP
.I /mnt/rootfs
Mount point supplied for the real root. The mounted filesystem must provide
proc, sys, and dev directories for the moved virtual filesystems.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/seed-sd
Descriptor-seeding helper invoked before hooks.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/sh
Recovery shell. This path must exist in the filesystem visible when recovery
is entered, including after a root switch.
.SH NOTES
Prelude reads the highest supported sequence version and validates its
first-line marker. A newer unsupported sequence alongside a supported one
produces a warning. A missing sequence or an unsupported-only set fails boot.
.PP
Old-root cleanup removes entries on the original device and preserves other
devices. Cleanup failure is a warning. Root handoff uses chroot rather than
pivot_root; a different device identifier is required at /mnt/rootfs.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mkirf (1),
.BR prelude-hooks (5),
.BR peinit2 (8),
.BR seed-sd (8)
